```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
No persistent misconfiguration exists in this topology. The connectivity
blackout reported by host_1_1_1_1 was a transient, self-healing event
caused by the normal OSPF convergence delay that occurred when the Kathara
lab was freshly started at 09:26:40 today.

Affected component: The OSPF routing process on switch_dist_1_1 (the
distribution-layer FRR router that serves as host_1_1_1_1's default
gateway at 10.1.1.1). This device carries no static routes; it relies
exclusively on OSPF to learn the 10.200.0.0/24 server subnet.

Mechanism: After lab startup, FRR began sending OSPF Hello packets on
eth0 (172.16.0.7/31), but the first Database Description negotiation
with router_core_1 did not complete until 09:27:25 — 45 seconds later
(confirmed in /var/log/frr/frr.log). Until that adjacency reached Full
state and SPF ran, switch_dist_1_1 had no route entry for 10.200.0.0/24.
Any packet host_1_1_1_1 sent toward 10.200.0.x arrived at the gateway
(10.1.1.1 / br0), found no matching route, and was silently dropped.

Localization: switch_dist_1_1, specifically its FRR ospfd process and
the absence of a static fallback route to 10.200.0.0/24.

Root cause: switch_dist_1_1 has no static route to the server subnet and
relies solely on OSPF, so the ~90-second OSPF convergence window after a
fresh lab start produced a complete blackout for host_1_1_1_1.

Corrective fix: The network has self-healed; OSPF has been fully
converged for over 11 minutes and all pings succeed. To prevent
recurrence on future lab restarts, add a static route on switch_dist_1_1:
  ip route 10.200.0.0/24 172.16.0.6
This ensures reachability during any future OSPF convergence window.
=== END ===
